@module sound ; The 48K beeper effects
@about On a 48K (Machine48K $7A76 set) PlayEffect $7A97 points PlayBeeperStep's note pointer at one of eleven effects, and the main loop calls PlayBeeperStep $7B66 once a pass: one note a pass, a square wave on port $FE bit 3 for $600 loop turns whatever the pitch. No recording runs a 48K, so this module is known only from the code; the static walk takes it for data, and it is written as DEFB, one instruction a line.

$7ABF c ReadMusicByte ; Read the RAM 1 music's byte $C9CF (A, flags of AND A); nothing on a 48K
> Not executed in any recording and called from nowhere in the image ($6000-$FEFD holds no CALL or JP to $7ABF). Pages RAM 1 in like CallRam1 and returns the byte $C9CF of the music (perhaps "a tune is playing"), interrupts left off.
> Witness: L1 0 ().
$7ABF ; LD A,($7A76): Machine48K
$7AC3 ; RET NZ: a 48K
$7ACA ; OUT (C),A: RAM 1 at $C000
$7AD5 ; OUT (C),A: RAM 0 back

$7ADA c SelectBeeperEffect ; 48K: PlayEffect's other half, the note pointer at $7B67 = BeeperEffectTable[A] for A < 11
> Reached from PlayEffect $7AA0 when Machine48K is set; ends with PlayEffect's POP BC, POP HL, POP AF, RET.
> Evidence: c48 (the 48K load and play, stage 2) 157 runs: every effect the play started.
> Witness: L1 157 (c48).
$7ADA ; CP $0B
$7ADC ; JP NC,$7AEE: no such effect
$7AE3 ; LD BC,$7AF2: BeeperEffectTable
$7AEA ; LD ($7B67),DE: PlayBeeperStep's note pointer
$7AEE SelectBeeperEffectDone

$7AF2 w BeeperEffectTable 1 ; The eleven beeper effects' addresses, by effect number (SelectBeeperEffect)

$7B08 b BeeperEffects ; The beeper effects: one note a pass, each byte the half period in loop turns, $FF ends
> Played by PlayBeeperStep $7B66 on a 48K. A lower byte is a higher note; every note lasts $600 loop turns (about 0.7 frame).
@res tables/beeper-effects.bin
$7B08 BeeperEffect0 ; 0: one note
$7B0A BeeperEffect1 ; 1: two notes
$7B0D BeeperEffect2 ; 2: one note
$7B0F BeeperEffect3 ; 3
$7B15 BeeperEffect4 ; 4: falling period, a rising sweep
$7B1C BeeperEffect5 ; 5: an alternating trill
$7B29 BeeperEffect6 ; 6: a warble
$7B3A BeeperEffect7 ; 7
$7B40 BeeperEffect8 ; 8: a rising period, 1-5
$7B46 BeeperEffect9 ; 9
$7B53 BeeperEffect10 ; 10: a long falling sweep

$7B66 c PlayBeeperStep ; 48K: play the next note of the current beeper effect, if any
> Called by the main loop $7D86 each pass when Machine48K is set. The note pointer (the LD HL operand at $7B67, set by SelectBeeperEffect) is 0 at the start: the first passes on a 48K would play the ROM's bytes at $0000 ($F3, $AF, $11) until its $FF at $0003. The note: C = the byte, then port $FE = $08 / $00 (MIC bit, black border) toggled every C turns of a DE = $600 countdown, about 49 000 T: most of a frame per pass. Not executed in any recording.
> Evidence: c48 1206 runs, one a main loop pass after the game started (no other recording is a 48K).
> Witness: L1 1206 (c48).
$7B66 ; LD HL,nn: nn = the next note
$7B6C ; RET Z: the effect is over
$7B71 ; LD C,A: the half period
$7B72 ; LD DE,$0600: the note's length
$7B75 BeeperHigh
$7B7A BeeperHighWait
$7B7E ; DJNZ BeeperHighWait
$7B80 BeeperLow
$7B85 BeeperLowWait
$7B89 ; DJNZ BeeperLowWait
$7B8B ; JP BeeperHigh

$7B8E b BeeperStepRet ; A RET after PlayBeeperStep's loop, which ends only by its RET Z: never reached
> One byte $C9 between PlayBeeperStep's JP BeeperHigh and the entry code at $7B8F: no instruction jumps to it, no recording reads or runs it (an older routine's end).

@isub $7C21 LD BC,35
@isub $7DDD LD DE,-$60
@isub $7DF4 LD DE,-$CC
@isub $7E7B LD DE,$20-$800
@isub $7E88 LD DE,$A00B
@isub $7E97 LD BC,512
@isub $7F55 LD DE,$700A
@isub $7F5E LD DE,$780A
@isub $7F67 LD DE,$9002
@isub $7F70 LD DE,$A002
@isub $7F79 LD DE,$B002
@isub $7F82 LD DE,$C002
@isub $7F94 LD DE,$D002
@isub $80C4 LD DE,$6807
@isub $80CF LD DE,$8009
@isub $80DA LD DE,$9809
@isub $80E5 LD DE,$A809
@isub $80F0 LD DE,$B809
@isub $80FB LD DE,$C809
@isub $8104 LD HL,Sprites+12*128
@isub $837F LD HL,SectionCounts-1
@isub $83D8 LD BC,-2
@isub $83F0 LD BC,-$30
@isub $846E LD DE,21760
@isub $877E LD BC,2500

@module setup ; The entry code: the 128K test, the music copy to RAM 1, the IM 2 set-up, the mirror table
@about The tape loader at $FF00 jumps to Entry ($7B8F) once side A is in. The code runs once: it sets SP to 0, installs the IM 2 handler, finds out whether a 128K's RAM 1 is there (a write to $C000 with RAM 1 paged in, read back with RAM 0), copies the music $6000-$707F into RAM 1, patches the handler to call the music player, builds the 256-byte bit-reverse table at $6500 and goes to the control menu.
@about Its bytes are then reused: ClearObjectTables ($83B8) zeroes $7B8F-$7C7C at every new game, and the area holds two tables of moving objects (six 24-byte records from $7B8F, end mark at $7C1F; four 23-byte records from $7C20, end mark at $7C7C) and, in the menus and the endings, the falling letters (8-byte records from $7B8F). The level start table LevelStarts at $7C8F is the only data here that stays.

$7B8F c Entry ; The game's entry: SP, the IM 2 handler, the 128K test (later the first object table)
> Called by the loader ($FF2A, JP) once, with interrupts off: SP = 0 (the stack grows down over the loader's tail), SetupIm2 copies the handler and sets IM 2. Then the 128K test: E = the byte at $C000 with RAM 0 paged in; RAM 1 is paged in (port $7FFD = $11: ROM 1, RAM 1), E + 1 written to $C000, RAM 0 paged back ($10) and $C000 read again. The same value as before means the write went to another bank: a 128K, go to CopyMusic. Otherwise the machine has one bank there: Entry48K puts the byte back, sets $7A76 = $FF (no music) and skips the copy.
> From $83B8 on, the bytes from here are the object table A (six 24-byte records, updated by $A2F7 from UpdateObjects, drawn by $7329 from DrawSprites, +0 = 0 a free record, $FF at $7C1F the end) and the falling letters of DropText (8-byte records, see BuildLetters).
> Evidence: c1 runs it once at frame 13 998 (the L1: reads $C000 and $6000-$707F, writes $C000-$D07F in RAM 1, $FF14-$FF16, $6500-$65FF, ports $7FFD and $FE). The 48K branch Entry48K is not executed in any recording (every recording is a 128K).
> Witness: L1 2 (c1, c48).

$7BB3 c Entry48K ; The 48K branch of the entry
> The code bytes of the 48K case: LD A,E / LD ($C000),A puts the test byte back (on a 48K the write went to the only bank), LD A,$FF / LD ($7A76),A turns the music off (the RAM 1 calls at $7A77 return at once), JP $7BF0 skips the music copy and the handler patch (the handler keeps its three NOPs at $FF14) and goes on with the bit-reverse table. Kept as bytes: no recording ran it, and the object tables write over it, so the walk takes it for data. A 48K run would settle it.
> Evidence: c48 once, at the entry after side A (the 48K load).
> Witness: L1 1 (c48).
$7BB3 ; LD A,E / LD ($C000),A: the test byte put back
$7BB7 ; LD A,$FF / LD ($7A76),A: 48K, no music
$7BBC ; JP $7BF0: past the music copy and the handler patch

$7BBF c CopyMusic ; 128K: copy the music to RAM 1, patch the handler to call the player, build the mirror table
> Copies the 4224 bytes $6000-$707F (the AY music as loaded) to $C000-$D07F in RAM 1, one byte at a time: the byte read with RAM 0 in, RAM 1 paged in ($7FFD = $11) for the write, RAM 0 back ($10); the source and destination pointers and the port are kept in the alternate registers. Then writes CALL $7AB6 over the three NOPs at $FF14-$FF16 of the IM 2 handler, so every interrupt runs the music player (through $7A77, which pages RAM 1 in). The 48K path joins at $7BF0: the 256-byte table at $6500, entry n = n with its bits reversed (the sprite blitter $7329 mirrors sprites through it, LD H,$65). Border black, EI, then the control menu (NewGame).
> Evidence: c1, once (writes $C000-$D07F in page 1, $FF14-$FF16, $6500-$65FF).
> Witness: L1 1 (c1).
$7BCD ; 4224 bytes
$7BD0 CopyMusicLoop ; one byte: read in RAM 0, written in RAM 1
$7BE5 ; CALL $7AB6 into the handler's three NOPs (Im2Handler+22)
$7BF0 BuildMirrorTable ; the 48K path joins here
$7BF4 BitReverseLoop ; C = B with its 8 bits reversed, stored at $6500 + B (B = 0, 255 ... 1)
$7C13 ; border black

$7C1A c SetupIm2 ; Copy the IM 2 handler to $FEFE, fill the vector table, IM 2
> Copies the 35 bytes of Im2HandlerSource ($7C39) to $FEFE-$FF20, fills $FD00-$FE00 (257 bytes) with $FE, sets I = $FD and IM 2: whatever byte the bus holds, the vector read is $FEFE. Returns with interrupts off (DI); Entry enables them at the end of CopyMusic.
> Evidence: c1, once, called from $7B93 (reads $7C39-$7C5B, writes $FD00-$FE00 and $FEFE-$FF20).
> Witness: L1 2 (c1, c48).
$7C2B FillIm2TableLoop ; 256 bytes $FE, then the 257th

@module interrupt ; The IM 2 handler's source, the interrupt's tick counter and the random numbers
@about The handler runs at $FEFE, copied there by SetupIm2 from Im2HandlerSource. Every interrupt it adds 1 to the frame counter $7A60 (which the frame wait $7A53 compares and clears), adds 1 to the 32-bit tick count Ticks (CountTicks), reads the controls into $7253 ($7254) and, on a 128K, runs the music player through $7AB6 (the CALL CopyMusic writes over its NOPs). The tick count feeds the random numbers of Random.

$7C39 b Im2HandlerSource ; The IM 2 handler as stored here; it runs at $FEFE (35 bytes)
> SetupIm2 copies these 35 bytes to $FEFE-$FF20 (LD HL,$7C39 / LD DE,$FEFE / LD BC,35 / LDIR); the code runs only there (in c1 4706 times, the IM 2 vector of every interrupt). DI; AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY pushed; the frame counter $7A60 + 1; CALL $81D4 (CountTicks); CALL $7254 (the controls into $7253, the method in $7252); three NOPs, which CopyMusic turns into CALL $7AB6 on a 128K (the music player, through the RAM 1 call $7A77); the registers popped; EI; RET. The handler lasts until its EI, so an interrupt that comes while code runs with interrupts off (the blitters, the map builder, the RAM 1 calls, the tape) is lost (WALKTHROUGH 1).
> After the copy the bytes here are part of the object table A (from $7B8F) and of the falling letters' records.
$7C39 ; DI, then PUSH AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY
$7C42 ; LD A,($7A60) / INC A / LD ($7A60),A: the frame counter
$7C49 ; CALL $81D4: CountTicks
$7C4C ; CALL $7254: read the controls
$7C4F ; NOP NOP NOP, at $FF14 in the copy: CALL $7AB6 (the music) on a 128K
$7C52 ; POP IY, IX, HL, DE, BC, AF
$7C5A ; EI / RET

$7C5C b EntryDebris ; Bytes of an earlier build after the handler (not executed in any recording)
> Not read as code by anything: they look like the tail of the handler of an earlier build, eight bytes higher (CALL $81DC, CALL $725C, the NOPs, the pops, EI, RET) and the start of an earlier NewGame (CALL $80B1 where this build has CALL $80B5 at $7C7D). The space belongs to the object table B ($7C20, four 23-byte records); ClearObjectTables writes its end mark $FF at $7C7C.
$7C7C ObjectTableBEnd ; the end mark of the object table B ($FF after ClearObjectTables)

@module mainloop ; The new game, the level start, the main loop and GAME OVER
@about The flow after the entry: NewGame shows the control menu, ChooseLevel the level menu, StartGame clears the object tables, resets the sections' objects and the score, LevelStart reads the level from tape when the one in memory is another, sets the lives, ammunition and detonators and Rick's start, and falls into MainLoop.
@about A pass of MainLoop: the quit key E (GAME OVER), two cheat key chords, the pause P, Rick and the objects updated ($8B42, UpdateObjects), the sprites composed (DrawSprites), the frame wait for 2 counts ($7A53, C = 2), the cells redrawn ($76D4), the section exit and the level's end, the scroll up or down, the death and the lives. A level's end moves to the next level or to an ending; the last life ends in GAME OVER and the control menu again.

$7C7D c NewGame ; A new game: the control menu, then the level menu
> Shows the control menu (ControlMenu) and returns when 0 (START GAME) is pressed; falls into ChooseLevel. Reached from CopyMusic once after the load and from GAME OVER ($7EB0) after every game.
> Evidence: c1 1, controls-keyboard 1, rzx-l5-full 2 (the start and the two GAME OVERs of the RZX level 5 run).
> Witness: L1 42 (controls-keyboard, rzx-l5-full, c1, c48, sec-l2-s7, sec-l2-s10-sec-l2-s12, sec-l3-s3-sec-l3-s5, sec-l3-s7-sec-l3-s9, sec-l4-s x5, sec-l5-s3, sec-l5-s5).

$7C80 c ChooseLevel ; The level menu
> LevelMenu: the level chosen goes to Level ($7EF1). Falls into StartGame.
> Evidence: 11 runs in 10 builds (c1, the four controls-*, the four load-*, rzx-l5-full 2).
> Witness: L1 54 (controls-cursor, controls-kempston, controls-keyboard, controls-sinclair, rzx-l5-full, sec-l1-s8, c1, c48, load-l2-load-l5, sec-l2-s7, sec-l2-s10-sec-l2-s12, sec-l3-s3-sec-l3-s5, sec-l3-s7-sec-l3-s9, sec-l4-s x5, sec-l5-s3, sec-l5-s5).

$7C83 c StartGame ; Clear the object tables, reset the sections' objects, start the game
> ClearObjectTables, ResetSectionObjects (bit 7 of every object record of every section cleared), then ResetGame, which runs the levels to GAME OVER and never returns.
> Evidence: 12 runs in 11 builds.
> Witness: L1 56 (controls-cursor, controls-kempston, controls-keyboard, controls-sinclair, rzx-l1-00000-poke, rzx-l5-full, sec-l1-s8, c1, c48, load-l2-load-l5, sec-l2-s7, sec-l2-s10-sec-l2-s12, sec-l3-s3-sec-l3-s5, sec-l3-s7-sec-l3-s9, sec-l4-s x5, sec-l5-s3, sec-l5-s5).

$7C8C c RestartJump ; JP NewGame after ResetGame
> JP $7C7D, back to the control menu: ResetGame never returns (GAME OVER jumps to NewGame itself), so this is not executed in any recording; the static walk takes it as the code after StartGame's CALL.
> Witness: L1 0 ().

$7C8F b LevelStarts 6 ; Rick's start in each level: section, row, x, y, a flag (6 bytes a level)
> LevelSetup takes the record of Level ($7EF1): IX = $7C8F + 6 x (level - 1). +0 the start section (A to the section setup $8A6D), +1 the view's top row in it ($88CF), +2 Rick's x (E: $9BAC, $9B8C), +3/+4 Rick's y, a word (HL: $9BAD, $9B8D), +5 a byte for $9BB1 (Rick's record +6: bit 7 the mirror; 0 in every level). Level 3 starts in section 1 with the view at row $90, level 5 at row $60, the others in section 0 at row 0; y is $CB in levels 1, 2, 4 and $AB in 3 and 5.
> Evidence: read by LevelSetup in every level start (reads $7C8F-$7CAC over the builds).
@res tables/level-starts.bin
$7C8F ; level 1: section 0, row 0, x 0, y $00CB
$7C95 ; level 2: section 0, row 0, x 0, y $00CB
$7C9B ; level 3: section 1, row $90, x 0, y $00AB
$7CA1 ; level 4: section 0, row 0, x 0, y $00CB
$7CA7 ; level 5: section 0, row $60, x 0, y $00AB

$7CAD c ResetGame ; A new game's state: score 0, levels done 0, six lives; then the levels
> Score ($7F1D-$7F22) = 0, LevelsDone ($7EF2) = 0, $8A24 = 0 (the level 5 code hooks off), lives ($8B3B) = 6. Falls into LevelStart and the main loop; never returns (GAME OVER goes to NewGame).
> Evidence: 12 runs in 11 builds, called from StartGame ($7C89).
> Witness: L1 56 (controls-cursor, controls-kempston, controls-keyboard, controls-sinclair, rzx-l1-00000-poke, rzx-l5-full, sec-l1-s8, c1, c48, load-l2-load-l5, sec-l2-s7, sec-l2-s10-sec-l2-s12, sec-l3-s3-sec-l3-s5, sec-l3-s7-sec-l3-s9, sec-l4-s x5, sec-l5-s3, sec-l5-s5).

$7CC5 c LevelStart ; Start the level in Level: from tape when another is in memory
> When Level ($7EF1) differs from LevelNumber ($C20C, the level in memory): tune 4 ($7AAD), $9BEB, LoadLevel (the tape messages and side B until the wanted level's block is in), then DecryptLevel. Reached from ResetGame and from MainLoop after a level's end ($7DD0).
> Evidence: 14 runs in 13 builds; the tape branch in the load-l2 ... load-l5 recordings and rzx-l3-10000-poke, rzx-l4-20000-poke (the RZX run's own loader, a TAP version at $846A).
> Witness: L1 60 (controls-cursor, controls-kempston, controls-keyboard, controls-sinclair, rzx-l1-00000-poke, rzx-l3-10000-poke, rzx-l4-20000-poke, rzx-l5-full, sec-l1-s8, c1, c48, load-l2-load-l5, sec-l2-s7, sec-l2-s10-sec-l2-s12, sec-l3-s3-sec-l3-s5, sec-l3-s7-sec-l3-s9, sec-l4-s x5, sec-l5-s3, sec-l5-s5).

$7CDB c DecryptLevel ; Decrypt the new level's last 768 bytes of maps
> CALL $894E (the maps' cipher). Then LevelTune.
> Evidence: 9 runs in 9 builds (load-l2 ... load-l5, rzx-l2/l3/l4/l5-00000-poke, rzx-l5-full).
> Witness: L1 48 (rzx-l2-00000-poke, rzx-l3-00000-poke, rzx-l4-00000-poke, rzx-l5-00000-poke, rzx-l5-full, load-l2-load-l5, sec-l2-s7, sec-l2-s10-sec-l2-s12, sec-l3-s3-sec-l3-s5, sec-l3-s7-sec-l3-s9, sec-l4-s x5, sec-l5-s3, sec-l5-s5).

$7CDE c LevelTune ; The level's tune; a life back when fewer than six
> Tune Level + 4 ($7AAD: 5 to 9). When lives ($8B3B) are not 6, AddLife adds one.
> Evidence: 17 runs in 15 builds; lives were 6 at every level start of the recordings (c1 starts a game, the RZX pieces have the infinite lives POKE), so AddLife is not executed in any recording.
> Witness: L1 100 (controls-cursor, controls-kempston, controls-keyboard, controls-sinclair, rzx-l1-00000-poke, rzx-l2-00000-poke, rzx-l3-00000-poke, rzx-l4-00000-poke, rzx-l5-00000-poke, rzx-l5-full, sec-l1-s8, c1, c48, load-l2-load-l5, sec-l2-s7, sec-l2-s10-sec-l2-s12, sec-l3-s3-sec-l3-s5, sec-l3-s7-sec-l3-s9, sec-l4-s x5, sec-l5-s3, sec-l5-s5).

$7CEE c AddLife ; INC A / LD ($8B3B),A: one life more at a level start
> The code bytes INC A / LD ($8B3B),A: lives + 1 when they are below 6. Not executed in any recording (every level start had six lives); a level start with five lives or fewer would run it.
> Witness: L1 0 ().

$7CF2 c LevelSetup ; Ammunition and detonators 6, Rick's start record, the first section
> Ammunition ($8B3C) and detonators ($8B3D) = 6, $945D, $9B99, $9B98 = 0. IX = LevelStarts + 6 x (Level - 1): $9BB1 = +5; A = +0 the section, C = +1 the top row, E = +2 x, HL = +3/+4 y, then the section setup $8A6D. Falls into MainLoop.
> Evidence: 17 runs in 15 builds.
> Witness: L1 100 (controls-cursor, controls-kempston, controls-keyboard, controls-sinclair, rzx-l1-00000-poke, rzx-l2-00000-poke, rzx-l3-00000-poke, rzx-l4-00000-poke, rzx-l5-00000-poke, rzx-l5-full, sec-l1-s8, c1, c48, load-l2-load-l5, sec-l2-s7, sec-l2-s10-sec-l2-s12, sec-l3-s3-sec-l3-s5, sec-l3-s7-sec-l3-s9, sec-l4-s x5, sec-l5-s3, sec-l5-s5).

$7D2D c MainLoop ; The main loop: quit, cheats, pause, the pass's work, the frame wait, scroll, death
> The quit key E ($724A, Z when pressed) goes to GameOver. $71C0 is Z when C, S and 3 are held together: CheatInfiniteKeys toggles CheatInfinite ($8066). Then MainLoopCheatB.
> Evidence: 50 163 runs in 65 builds (c1 1114, controls-keyboard 465, ...). E in c1 and controls-keyboard went to GAME OVER. The chord C+S+3: cheats (stage 2).
> Witness: L1 119172 (cheats, controls-cursor, controls-kempston, controls-keyboard, controls-sinclair, end-congrats, long-l4, rzx-l1-00000-poke, rzx-l1-02000-poke, rzx-l1-04000-poke, rzx-l1-06000-poke, rzx-l1-08000-poke, rzx-l1-10000-poke, rzx-l1-12000-poke, rzx-l1-14000-poke, rzx-l1-16000-poke, rzx-l1-18000-poke, rzx-l1-20000-poke, rzx-l1-22000-poke, rzx-l1-24000-poke, rzx-l2-00000-poke, rzx-l2-02000-poke, rzx-l2-04000-poke, rzx-l2-06000-poke, rzx-l2-08000-poke, rzx-l2-10000-poke, rzx-l2-12000-poke, rzx-l2-14000-poke, rzx-l2-16000-poke, rzx-l2-18000-poke, rzx-l2-20000-poke, rzx-l2-22000-poke, rzx-l2-24000-poke, rzx-l3-00000-poke, rzx-l3-02000-poke, rzx-l3-04000-poke, rzx-l3-06000-poke, rzx-l3-08000-poke, rzx-l3-10000-poke, rzx-l4-00000-poke, rzx-l4-02000-poke, rzx-l4-04000-poke, rzx-l4-06000-poke, rzx-l4-08000-poke, rzx-l4-10000-poke, rzx-l4-12000-poke, rzx-l4-14000-poke, rzx-l4-16000-poke, rzx-l4-18000-poke, rzx-l4-20000-poke, rzx-l5-00000-poke, rzx-l5-02000-poke, rzx-l5-04000-poke, rzx-l5-06000-poke, rzx-l5-08000-poke, rzx-l5-10000-poke, rzx-l5-12000-poke, rzx-l5-14000-poke, rzx-l5-16000-poke, rzx-l5-18000-poke, rzx-l5-20000-poke, rzx-l5-22000-poke, rzx-l5-full, sec-l1-s8, c1, c48, load-l2-load-l5, sec-l2-s7, sec-l2-s10-sec-l2-s12, sec-l3-s3-sec-l3-s5, sec-l3-s7-sec-l3-s9, sec-l4-s x5, sec-l5-s3, sec-l5-s5).

$7D39 c CheatInfiniteKeys ; C+S+3: toggle CheatInfinite
> The code bytes LD A,1 / CALL $7AAD (tune 1, the signal) / LD A,($8066) / XOR $FF / LD ($8066),A. CheatInfinite then stops the loss of lives ($8A26), ammunition ($9593) and detonators ($9B27, the reads of $8066).
> Evidence: cheats (stage 2: C, S and 3 held at frame 50 on the RZX run's level 5) 3 runs: the chord held 10 frames toggles three times in its first passes.
> Witness: L1 6 (cheats).
$7D39 ; LD A,1 / CALL $7AAD: tune 1
$7D3E ; LD A,($8066) / XOR $FF / LD ($8066),A: CheatInfinite toggled

$7D46 c MainLoopCheatB ; The second cheat chord: SPACE, N, ENTER, J and Y
> $71DC is Z when SPACE, N, ENTER, J and Y are held together; then CheatExitsKeys. Then MainLoopPause.
> Evidence: 50 161 runs in 65 builds; the chord: cheats (stage 2).
> Witness: L1 119134 (cheats, controls-cursor, controls-kempston, controls-keyboard, controls-sinclair, end-congrats, long-l4, rzx-l1-00000-poke, rzx-l1-02000-poke, rzx-l1-04000-poke, rzx-l1-06000-poke, rzx-l1-08000-poke, rzx-l1-10000-poke, rzx-l1-12000-poke, rzx-l1-14000-poke, rzx-l1-16000-poke, rzx-l1-18000-poke, rzx-l1-20000-poke, rzx-l1-22000-poke, rzx-l1-24000-poke, rzx-l2-00000-poke, rzx-l2-02000-poke, rzx-l2-04000-poke, rzx-l2-06000-poke, rzx-l2-08000-poke, rzx-l2-10000-poke, rzx-l2-12000-poke, rzx-l2-14000-poke, rzx-l2-16000-poke, rzx-l2-18000-poke, rzx-l2-20000-poke, rzx-l2-22000-poke, rzx-l2-24000-poke, rzx-l3-00000-poke, rzx-l3-02000-poke, rzx-l3-04000-poke, rzx-l3-06000-poke, rzx-l3-08000-poke, rzx-l3-10000-poke, rzx-l4-00000-poke, rzx-l4-02000-poke, rzx-l4-04000-poke, rzx-l4-06000-poke, rzx-l4-08000-poke, rzx-l4-10000-poke, rzx-l4-12000-poke, rzx-l4-14000-poke, rzx-l4-16000-poke, rzx-l4-18000-poke, rzx-l4-20000-poke, rzx-l5-00000-poke, rzx-l5-02000-poke, rzx-l5-04000-poke, rzx-l5-06000-poke, rzx-l5-08000-poke, rzx-l5-10000-poke, rzx-l5-12000-poke, rzx-l5-14000-poke, rzx-l5-16000-poke, rzx-l5-18000-poke, rzx-l5-20000-poke, rzx-l5-22000-poke, rzx-l5-full, sec-l1-s8, c1, c48, load-l2-load-l5, sec-l2-s7, sec-l2-s10-sec-l2-s12, sec-l3-s3-sec-l3-s5, sec-l3-s7-sec-l3-s9, sec-l4-s x5, sec-l5-s3, sec-l5-s5).

$7D4C c CheatExitsKeys ; The chord with level 5 open: CheatExits on
> The code bytes LD A,($8065) / AND A / JP Z,$7D5D / LD A,1 / CALL $7AAD / LD A,$FF / LD ($8067),A: only when Level5Open is set, tune 1 and CheatExits ($8067) = $FF. The section exit test $8993 reads CheatExits (bit 7) before it tests bit 4 of an exit's kind ($89D9): which exits it opens is for the run. Not executed in any recording.
> Evidence: cheats (stage 2: SPACE, N, ENTER, J and Y held at frame 200, Level5Open set by the RZX run) 3 runs; long-l4 (POKE CheatExits = $FF on the RZX run's level 4 end) shows what it opens: LongLevelsTest sends the level-ending exit $82 of level 4 section 7 on to section 10.
> Witness: L1 6 (cheats).
$7D4C ; LD A,($8065) / AND A / JP Z,$7D5D: only with level 5 open
$7D53 ; LD A,1 / CALL $7AAD: tune 1
$7D58 ; LD A,$FF / LD ($8067),A: CheatExits on

$7D5D c MainLoopPause ; The pause, the pass's work, the frame wait, the level's end, scroll, death; GAME OVER
> P ($7242, Z when pressed): wait for its release, a press and a release (the game stands still, the interrupt goes on with the music and the controls). Then the pass: $8B42 (Rick from the controls), ExitDirection ($88D3) = 0, UpdateObjects, DrawSprites; on a 48K ($7A76 not 0) CALL $7B66; the frame wait $7A53 with C = 2 (the pass takes at least two frames); the cells redrawn ($76D4).
> The level's end: when a routine set ExitDirection, the section exit test $8993; its result negative (M) is the level's end. LevelsDone ($7EF2) + 1; when it was 3 (the fourth level done in this game), Level5Open ($8065) = $FF. LevelsDone = 5 goes to BonusTally (the Fat Guy's end); Level 5 ended otherwise goes to Congratulations; level 4 goes to Congratulations unless Level5Open is set, then to level 5; levels 1-3 to the next. Then LevelStart.
> The scroll: unless $9B9C is set, Rick's y ($9BAD) below $60 with the view not at the top ($88CF) scrolls up ($7905); y at $CC or more with the top row below ViewLastRow ($88D2) scrolls down ($78A5).
> The death: when $9B9B is set, the last life (lives = 1) is GAME OVER, else $8A26 (a life off, the section again).
> GameOver ($7E1E): tune 2, the cell drawer's JP M at $775A turned into NOPs, $8985, the view buffer $6D60-$6DDF cleared, a 12 x 3 box at column 10, rows 11-13 of the screen: attributes $47, pixels 0; "GAME  OVER" falls into place (DropText at row $A0, column 11) and is printed on the screen ($7F23 at $488B); wait 512 frames or fire, SPACE, ENTER ($7229); LevelCellsMode, NewGame.
> Evidence: the pass 50 161 runs in 65 builds. The pause in c1 (7476 runs of PauseWaitRelease) and controls-keyboard. The level's end: $7DA6 (level 5 opened) once in rzx-l4-20000-poke, the next level ($7DCC) in rzx-l3-10000-poke and rzx-l4-20000-poke; BonusTally and Congratulations not from this code in any recording (the RZX run's ending, rzx-l5-23000, starts inside TallyLives). GameOver 4 runs: c1 1 and controls-keyboard 1 (the key E), rzx-l5-full 2 (the lives lost).
> Witness: L1 119134 (cheats, controls-cursor, controls-kempston, controls-keyboard, controls-sinclair, end-congrats, long-l4, rzx-l1-00000-poke, rzx-l1-02000-poke, rzx-l1-04000-poke, rzx-l1-06000-poke, rzx-l1-08000-poke, rzx-l1-10000-poke, rzx-l1-12000-poke, rzx-l1-14000-poke, rzx-l1-16000-poke, rzx-l1-18000-poke, rzx-l1-20000-poke, rzx-l1-22000-poke, rzx-l1-24000-poke, rzx-l2-00000-poke, rzx-l2-02000-poke, rzx-l2-04000-poke, rzx-l2-06000-poke, rzx-l2-08000-poke, rzx-l2-10000-poke, rzx-l2-12000-poke, rzx-l2-14000-poke, rzx-l2-16000-poke, rzx-l2-18000-poke, rzx-l2-20000-poke, rzx-l2-22000-poke, rzx-l2-24000-poke, rzx-l3-00000-poke, rzx-l3-02000-poke, rzx-l3-04000-poke, rzx-l3-06000-poke, rzx-l3-08000-poke, rzx-l3-10000-poke, rzx-l4-00000-poke, rzx-l4-02000-poke, rzx-l4-04000-poke, rzx-l4-06000-poke, rzx-l4-08000-poke, rzx-l4-10000-poke, rzx-l4-12000-poke, rzx-l4-14000-poke, rzx-l4-16000-poke, rzx-l4-18000-poke, rzx-l4-20000-poke, rzx-l5-00000-poke, rzx-l5-02000-poke, rzx-l5-04000-poke, rzx-l5-06000-poke, rzx-l5-08000-poke, rzx-l5-10000-poke, rzx-l5-12000-poke, rzx-l5-14000-poke, rzx-l5-16000-poke, rzx-l5-18000-poke, rzx-l5-20000-poke, rzx-l5-22000-poke, rzx-l5-full, sec-l1-s8, c1, c48, load-l2-load-l5, sec-l2-s7, sec-l2-s10-sec-l2-s12, sec-l3-s3-sec-l3-s5, sec-l3-s7-sec-l3-s9, sec-l4-s x5, sec-l5-s3, sec-l5-s5).
$7D63 PauseWaitRelease ; P still held
$7D69 PauseWaitPress ; paused: wait for P
$7D6F PauseWaitRelease2 ; wait for P's release, then play on
$7D75 PassWork ; the pass's work
$7D79 ; no exit asked yet in this pass
$7D82 ; a 48K: CALL $7B66
$7D89 ; two frame counts a pass
$7D8E ; the cells that changed redrawn
$7D98 ; the section exit test: M = the level's end
$7DA6 ; the fourth level done in this game: level 5 opens
$7DAD CountLevelDone ; LevelsDone + 1; 5 is the Fat Guy's end
$7DCC NextLevel ; Level + 1, the next level's start
$7DD3 ScrollTest ; scroll up when Rick is high in the view
$7DF1 ScrollDownTest ; scroll down when Rick is low in the view
$7E09 DeathTest ; Rick dead: GAME OVER on the last life, else a life off
$7E1E GameOver ; GAME OVER (the last life lost, or the key E)
$7E23 ; NOP NOP NOP over the JP M at $775A
$7E2F ; 129 bytes of the view buffer from $6D60 cleared (under the box)
$7E63 ; the box's pixels: 3 rows of 8 lines, 12 bytes
$7E68 GameOverClearRow ; a character row of the box
$7E6A GameOverClearLine ; a pixel line of the box
$7E7B ; back 8 lines, down a character row
$7E83 ; "GAME  OVER" falls into the view (A = $FF)
$7E8E ; and is printed on the screen
$7E9A GameOverWait ; 512 frames or fire
$7EAD GameOverDone ; the level cells back, the control menu

@module render ; The cell drawer switched between the level's cells and the font
@about The cell drawer $7755-$77A2 reads its cell graphics from the base stored at $7730 and $7777 and leaves the cells whose flag has bit 7 by the JP M at $775A. In the menus and the endings FontCellsMode points it at the font $7080 and turns the JP M into NOPs (no flags: the cell numbers are glyph numbers); LevelCellsMode puts the level's cells $A900 and the JP M back.

$7EB3 c LevelCellsMode ; The cell drawer back to the level's cells
> Writes $A900 (CellGraphics) to the drawer's two bases $7730 and $7777 and JP M,$77A2 back at $775A. The same code ends LevelMenu ($7FB2).
> Evidence: 3 runs in 2 builds (controls-keyboard 1, rzx-l5-full 2): after GAME OVER ($7EAD).
> Witness: L1 40 (controls-keyboard, rzx-l5-full, sec-l2-s7, sec-l2-s10-sec-l2-s12, sec-l3-s3-sec-l3-s5, sec-l3-s7-sec-l3-s9, sec-l4-s x5, sec-l5-s3, sec-l5-s5).

@module ending ; GAME OVER's text
@about GAME OVER (the code is GameOver, $7E1E, in MainLoopPause) shows this text twice: falling into the view and then printed on the screen.

$7EC8 t GameOverText ; "GAME  OVER", $FF the end
> Read by DropText (from $7E8B) and PrintText (from $7E94). The font holds the digits first: a character is drawn as glyph (code - $34), so letters are plain ASCII and a digit is stored 4 higher.
@res texts/game-over.bin

@module score ; The score: the points records, the score digits, AddScore
@about The score is six decimal digits, one a byte, the most significant first (Score, $7F1D-$7F22). AddScore adds a record of six digits in the same form; the records hold the points of each event. The HUD's score is drawn by $7A0C.

$7ED3 c AddScore ; Add six decimal digits at HL to the score, draw it
> HL = a points record (six digits, the most significant first). From the last digit back: score digit + record digit + carry; 10 or more takes 10 off with the carry set. Then $7A0C draws the score. Changes A, B, DE, HL.
> The callers: $8C36 and $9F6D (Points50), $A595 (Points500), $9CD6 (TimeBonus), TallyLives (Points100000) and level 5's own code at $F964; BonusTally (not run) adds Points100000, then Points5000 per bullet and Points10000 per detonator.
> Evidence: 149 runs in 47 builds (callers $8736 x5, $8C36 x5, $9CD6 x9, $9F6D x101, $A595 x28, $F964 x1); the RZX memory at level 5, frame 1000 holds 00 04 01 03 08 00 with "041380" on the screen (stage 1).
> Witness: L1 310 (cheats, rzx-l1-00000-poke, rzx-l1-02000-poke, rzx-l1-06000-poke, rzx-l1-08000-poke, rzx-l1-10000-poke, rzx-l1-12000-poke, rzx-l1-14000-poke, rzx-l1-16000-poke, rzx-l1-24000-poke, rzx-l2-00000-poke, rzx-l2-02000-poke, rzx-l2-04000-poke, rzx-l2-06000-poke, rzx-l2-08000-poke, rzx-l2-10000-poke, rzx-l2-12000-poke, rzx-l2-14000-poke, rzx-l2-16000-poke, rzx-l2-18000-poke, rzx-l2-20000-poke, rzx-l2-22000-poke, rzx-l3-00000-poke, rzx-l3-02000-poke, rzx-l3-04000-poke, rzx-l3-06000-poke, rzx-l3-08000-poke, rzx-l3-10000-poke, rzx-l4-00000-poke, rzx-l4-02000-poke, rzx-l4-04000-poke, rzx-l4-06000-poke, rzx-l4-08000-poke, rzx-l4-14000-poke, rzx-l4-16000-poke, rzx-l4-18000-poke, rzx-l5-00000-poke, rzx-l5-02000-poke, rzx-l5-04000-poke, rzx-l5-06000-poke, rzx-l5-10000-poke, rzx-l5-12000-poke, rzx-l5-16000-poke, rzx-l5-20000-poke, rzx-l5-22000-poke, rzx-l5-23000, rzx-l5-full, load-l5, sec-l2-s7, sec-l2-s12, sec-l5-s3).
$7EDC AddScoreLoop ; one digit, with the carry from the one after it
$7EE7 AddScoreDigit ; the digit stored, the carry for the next

@module mainloop
$7EF1 b Level ; The level to play, 1-5
> Set by LevelMenu (the key pressed) and by MainLoopPause at a level's end (Level + 1, or 5). LevelStart compares it with LevelNumber ($C20C); LevelSetup indexes LevelStarts with it, ResetSectionObjects SectionCounts, Congratulations writes it into the text; read by $95ED, $96B4, $988F, $9B07 too. 1 in the image.

$7EF2 b LevelsDone ; The levels completed in this game
> 0 at ResetGame; + 1 at each level's end (MainLoopPause $7DAD). Done with 3 (the fourth level of the game) it opens level 5 (Level5Open); 5 (all five in one game, so starting from level 1) is the Fat Guy's end, BonusTally.

@module score
$7EF3 b Points50 6 ; 50 points (000050)
> Added by $8C36 and $9F6D.
@res tables/points-50.bin

$7EF9 b Points100 6 ; 100 points (000100)
> No instruction in the image or the levels loads $7EF9 (no byte pair F9 7E in the 64 K image), and no recording reads it: unused.
@res tables/points-100.bin

$7EFF b Points500 6 ; 500 points (000500)
> Added by $A595.
@res tables/points-500.bin

$7F05 b Points5000 6 ; 5000 points (005000): each bullet left at the Fat Guy's end
> Added by BonusTally ($86F1) for each bullet left (not executed in any recording).
@res tables/points-5000.bin

$7F0B b Points10000 6 ; 10 000 points (010000): each detonator left at the Fat Guy's end
> Added by BonusTally ($8712) for each detonator left (not executed in any recording).
@res tables/points-10000.bin

$7F11 b Points100000 6 ; 100 000 points (100000): the Fat Guy defeated, and each life left
> Added once by BonusTally ($86E0) and once a life by TallyLives (rzx-l5-23000: 5 additions).
@res tables/points-100000.bin

$7F17 b TimeBonus 6 ; A bonus that counts down: 002000 when set, collected by $9CD6
> $9C97 sets its digits +2..+4 to 2, 0, 0 (2000) when Rick takes a certain object (the code at $9CAC), $9163 takes 1 off its digits +1..+4 every 25 passes while $9BA9 is set, and $9CCA adds what is left to the score (AddScore, HL = $7F17).

$7F1D b Score 6 ; The score: six decimal digits, the most significant first
> Cleared by ResetGame, added to by AddScore, drawn by $7A0C.

@module hud ; A text printed on the screen in the HUD font
@about PrintText draws a text straight on the screen with the font at $7080 ($7A2C a glyph): the tape messages and GAME OVER's second form.

$7F23 c PrintText ; Print a text on the screen: HL the text ($FF the end), DE the screen address
> Each character: $FF ends, a space moves on a column, else the glyph $7080 + 8 x (code - $34) is drawn at DE by $7A2C; DE + 1. So the digits are stored 4 higher ("4" is the glyph 0). Out: HL on the $FF.
> Evidence: 512 runs in 9 builds (callers $7E94 x4 GAME OVER, $8415 x20 and $8430 x18 the tape messages, a run a character).
> Witness: L1 976 (controls-keyboard, rzx-l3-10000-poke, rzx-l4-20000-poke, rzx-l5-full, c1, c48, load-l2-load-l5, sec-l2-s7, sec-l2-s10-sec-l2-s12, sec-l3-s3-sec-l3-s5, sec-l3-s7-sec-l3-s9, sec-l4-s x5, sec-l5-s3, sec-l5-s5).
$7F40 PrintTextNext ; the next column

@module menu ; The control and level menus, the cheat flags, the falling letters
@about The menus are drawn in the view buffer with the font as cells (FontCellsMode): each text falls into place letter by letter (DropText: BuildLetters makes a record a letter, MoveLetters makes them fall and bounce, ComposeLetter puts them into spare cells), until all have stopped or a number key is pressed; a key during the fall stops the animation, and the following texts are printed at once. ReadMenuKey takes the number keys 0-5 (5 only with level 5 open), a key only once per press.
@about The control menu (ControlMenu) lists START GAME and the four control methods; a small Rick slides to the method chosen. The level menu (LevelMenu) lists levels 1-4 and level 5 when Level5Open is set (four levels done in one game, MainLoopPause).

$7F44 c LevelMenu ; The level menu: levels 1-4, level 5 when open; Level = the key pressed
> FontCellsMode, $7A61, $8985, $8977, then the texts with DropText (A = $FF the first time, then what it returns): "PRESS KEY TO" at row $70 column 10, "START  LEVEL" at $78/10, the four levels at rows $90, $A0, $B0, $C0 column 2, and "THE FAT GUYS HEADQUARTERS" at $D0 only when Level5Open is set. A key 1-5 pressed during the fall is taken at once; else LevelMenuWait waits for one. Level = the key; LevelCellsMode's code inline; return to StartGame.
> Evidence: 11 runs in 10 builds; the menu after side A lists levels 1-4 (load-l5's first frames before its POKE was added, stage 1), load-l5 with the POKE $8065 = $FF shows level 5 and plays it.
> Witness: L1 54 (controls-cursor, controls-kempston, controls-keyboard, controls-sinclair, rzx-l5-full, sec-l1-s8, c1, c48, load-l2-load-l5, sec-l2-s7, sec-l2-s10-sec-l2-s12, sec-l3-s3-sec-l3-s5, sec-l3-s7-sec-l3-s9, sec-l4-s x5, sec-l5-s3, sec-l5-s5).
$7F55 ; D = the row in pixels, E = the column
$7F91 ; the fifth level: only with Level5Open

$7F9D c LevelMenuWait ; Wait for a level key
> ReadMenuKey until it gives 1-5 (0 and none go round again).
> Evidence: 82 475 runs in 2 builds (rzx-l1-00000-poke 37 177, rzx-l5-full 45 298: the RZX states sit in the level menu).
> Witness: L1 164950 (rzx-l1-00000-poke, rzx-l5-full).
$7FA0 LevelMenuKey ; FF (none) or 0: wait; 1-5: the level
$7FAF LevelMenuAgain ; no level key yet
$7FB2 LevelMenuDone ; the level cells back (as LevelCellsMode)

$7FC7 t LevelMenuText ; The level menu's texts (the digits stored 4 higher: "5" shows as 1)
> Each text ends with $FF. Read by DropText from LevelMenu; the level names are Rick Dangerous 2's five levels.
@res texts/level-menu.bin
$7FD4 StartLevelText ; "START  LEVEL"
$7FE1 Level1Name ; "5  HYDE PARK LONDON": 1
$7FF5 Level2Name ; "6  THE ICE CAVERNS OF FREEZIA": 2
$8013 Level3Name ; "7  THE FORESTS OF VEGETABLIA": 3
$8030 Level4Name ; "8  THE ATOMIC MUD MINES": 4
$8048 Level5Name ; "9  THE FAT GUYS HEADQUARTERS": 5, shown only when Level5Open

$8065 b Level5Open ; $FF: level 5 is open in the level menu
> Set to $FF by MainLoopPause ($7DA6) when the fourth level of a game is done; never cleared, so it stays open for the next games until a reset. Read by LevelMenu (the fifth line), ScanNumberKeys (the key 5), MainLoopPause (level 4 done: level 5 or the CONGRATULATIONS end) and CheatExitsKeys. 0 in the image. Evidence: rzx-l4-20000-poke runs $7DA6 once; load-l5 sets it by POKE.

$8066 b CheatInfinite ; Not 0: lives, ammunition and detonators are not used up
> Toggled by the chord C+S+3 in MainLoop (CheatInfiniteKeys); read by $8A26 (a life lost), $9593 (a bullet), $9B27 (a detonator), $95ED and $96B4. 0 in the image; no recording sets it.

$8067 b CheatExits ; $FF: a section exit test passes (set by the chord SPACE+N+ENTER+J+Y with level 5 open)
> Set by CheatExitsKeys, read by $8993 (bit 7, at $89D9); never cleared. 0 in the image; no recording sets it.

$8068 c ReadMenuKey ; A number key 0-5 once per press: A = the key, or $FF
> ScanNumberKeys; none: MenuKeyHeld = 0 and A = $FF. A key while MenuKeyHeld is set (still held from the last time): A = $FF. Else MenuKeyHeld = $FF and A = the key. Out: A.
> Evidence: 89 436 runs in 13 builds (callers LevelMenuWait, ControlMenuLoop, DropTextWait).
> Witness: L1 198492 (controls-cursor, controls-kempston, controls-keyboard, controls-sinclair, end-congrats, rzx-l1-00000-poke, rzx-l5-23000, rzx-l5-24000-poke, rzx-l5-full, sec-l1-s8, c1, c48, load-l2-load-l5, sec-l2-s7, sec-l2-s10-sec-l2-s12, sec-l3-s3-sec-l3-s5, sec-l3-s7-sec-l3-s9, sec-l4-s x5, sec-l5-s3, sec-l5-s5).
