@module traps ; The traps and pickups: paths, animations, the deadly touch, triggers, the three pickups
@about The traps live in the 24-byte records at $7B8F (to the $FF end mark), run once a pass by UpdateTrap from $865D: +0 the kind (bit 0 alive, bit 1 path paused, bits 2-4 the trigger behaviour, bit 5, bit 6 deadly, bit 7 the detonator test), +1 x, +2/+3 y, +4/+5 the sprite (0: none), +6 flags (bit 7 mirror, 6 no trigger test, 5 freed at the path's end, 4 waiting for the path's end to switch, 3 one more wait), +7 width, +8 height, +9 the frame delay, +$0A/+$0B the animation script, +$0C/+$0D the map entry, +$0E the path step count, +$0F/+$10 the path, +$11/+$12 the second path, +$13/+$14 the second animation, +$15 the second kind, +$16 the last x step, +$17 the last y step.
@about A path is a list of steps: a count and a byte of two signed nibbles (x step high, y step low; 7 means 8), done count times; $FF n plays effect n; 0 and an address goes on there (the loop point, where a waiting trap switches to its second path). An animation script is frame bytes (a frame number for SpriteFrameMap, $7F hidden), $80+delay then a frame (shown delay passes), $FD n an effect, $FF and an address a jump.

$A2E1 c TrapEffect ; Play effect A when the record at IX is in the shown part (y $40-$FF)
> For the effects a path ($A327, 115 runs) or an animation script ($A631, 1239 runs) asks for: off the shown part nothing is heard. Witness: 1354 runs in 47 builds, c1 53; 258 times silent ($A2F5).
